  <Groups>
    <Group elementId="CPLX-7214" textLabel="CPLX-7214" type="Complex">
      <Xref identifier="CPLX-7214" dataSource="plantcyc" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="215" width="140" height="100" textColor="666666" fontName="Arial" fontSize="10" borderColor="9900CC" borderStyle="Dashed" borderWidth="2.0" fillColor="F5E6FF" shapeType="Rectangle" zOrder="-1" />
      <Comment source="THE-ARABIDOPSIS-INFORMATION-RESOURCE, hartmut">The enzyme catalyzing the second and final step in the glutathione biosynthesis, i.e. glutathione synthetase has been isolated and characterized from &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt; |CITS: [8612643]| |CITS: [8914526]| |CITS: [15302873]|. Glutathione synthetase is a physiological dimer and catalyzes the ATP-dependent conversion of L-&amp;gamma;-glutamylcysteine to glutathione. The reaction mechanism was found to follow a random ter-reactant kinetic with a preferred ordered pathway |CITS: [15302873]|. The molecular function was confirmed by expressing and characterizing the recombinant protein in &lt;i&gt;E. coli&lt;/i&gt; |CITS: [15302873]| and by functional complementation of glutathione synthase deficient &lt;i&gt;Saccharomyces cerevisiae&lt;/i&gt; |CITS: [8612643]| and fission yeast (&lt;i&gt;Schizosaccharomyces pombe&lt;/i&gt;) |CITS: [8914526]|. Although this enzyme can be detected in the chloroplast, it seems to be primarily located in the cytoplasm |CITS:[15610346]|. And there is evidence that normal plant development can occur when GSH synthesis is limited to the cytosol |CITS: [18088327]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="CPLX-7214" />
